AISHELL-1 ASR baseline subset -- LICENSE and attribution
=======================================================

Everything under this directory is a derived subset of two Apache-2.0 licensed
speech corpora published by OpenSLR. The full text of the Apache License,
Version 2.0 follows verbatim after this notice (11,358 bytes; sha256
cfc7749b96f63bd31c3c42b5c471bf756814053e847c10f3eb003417bc523d30, fetched from
https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0.txt).

Attribution -- upstream works
-----------------------------

1) AISHELL-1  (source of every waveform's speech, and of every reference text)
   OpenSLR resource SLR33 -- https://www.openslr.org/33/
   Archive: https://openslr.org/resources/33/data_aishell.tgz
            15,582,913,665 bytes, Last-Modified 2017-10-03
   Reference: Bu, Du, Na, Wu, Zheng, "AISHELL-1: An open-source Mandarin speech
   corpus and a speech recognition baseline", ISCSLP 2016.
   Upstream license label (verbatim from the SLR33 page):
       License: Apache License v.2.0
   The same page also states, verbatim: "The data is free for academic use."
   That sentence is inconsistent with the Apache-2.0 label. It is recorded here
   rather than resolved; see docs/evidence/s1/cer-a1-research.md section 6 and
   the DEFERRED question raised there.

2) THCHS-30   (source of the additive noise only -- no THCHS-30 speech is used)
   OpenSLR resource SLR18 -- https://www.openslr.org/18/
   Archive: https://openslr.org/resources/18/resource.tgz
            24,813,708 bytes
   Only resource/noise/{cafe,car,white}/{cafe,car,white}.wav are used
   (16 kHz, mono, 16-bit PCM, 300.0 s each).
   Reference: Wang, Zhang, Zhang, "THCHS-30: A Free Chinese Speech Corpus",
   arXiv:1512.01882.
   Upstream license label (verbatim from the SLR18 page):
       License: Apache License v.2.0

Modifications (Apache-2.0 section 4(b) -- "changed files")
---------------------------------------------------------

This directory is a MODIFIED subset, not a verbatim redistribution:

* Subsetting: 100 utterances were selected from the AISHELL-1 *test* split out
  of 7,176. File selection, transcription normalization and every mixing
  parameter are fixed in tools/make_asr_baseline.py and are reproducible.
* near_clean/ (60 utterances): the upstream wav files are copied BYTE FOR BYTE.
  AISHELL-1 is natively 16 kHz / mono / 16-bit PCM, so there is no transcoding
  and no resampling; each file's sha256 equals its upstream counterpart's.
  Only the file name and the companion .txt (normalized reference text) are new.
* noisy_far/ (40 utterances): the upstream clean wav is mixed with a 300 s
  THCHS-30 noise excerpt at a target SNR of 10 dB, then filtered with a 6 kHz
  Blackman-windowed-sinc FIR low-pass (129 taps) and attenuated by 6 dB.
  THIS NOISE IS SIMULATED. It is not a real far-field recording and must not be
  described as one.

Known upstream ambiguity
------------------------

OpenSLR publishes no checksums for either archive, so integrity cannot be
verified against the publisher. This directory therefore records the sha256 of
every file it ships (sources.tsv). For near_clean those values equal the
sha256 of the matching upstream AISHELL-1 files, because those files are
byte-identical copies; for noisy_far the value identifies the derived file.
Near_clean's source utterance id is recorded per row, so the derivation can be
re-checked against a local copy of the corpus.

